{"text": " And the 60th U.S. Secretary of the Treasury, David Kennedy, reportedly drew on the fund to buy an Oriental carpet for $8,000. The answer is a pot of money at the Treasury called the Exchange Stabilization Fund. The Secretary of the Treasury can spend it without asking Congress. He just needs the President's signature, and the credit card opens up. It was created with one goal. It's in the name, to stabilize the exchange value of the dollar. But throughout history, that's not what it tends to get used for. The word stabilize wasn't as clearly defined as it should have been, so the Exchange Stabilization Fund tends to get spent on whatever the Secretary decides. When the 63rd Secretary of the Treasury, William Simon, took a trip to London, Moscow, and Mexico City with 42 people on it, that cost $131,000 and had no official purpose anyone could identify, once again paid for by the Exchange Stabilization Fund. You can think of it as the Secretary of the Treasury and President's personal piggy bank. The statute also says his decisions are final and, I'm quoting, may not be reviewed by another officer or employee of the government.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.28, "text": " And the 60th U.S. Secretary of the Treasury, David Kennedy,", "tokens": [50363, 843, 262, 3126, 400, 471, 13, 50, 13, 4986, 286, 262, 13419, 11, 3271, 10401, 11, 50535], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14298798487736628, "compression_ratio": 1.6346153846153846, "no_speech_prob": 0.0007915947353467345, "words": [{"word": " And", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.14, "probability": 0.1662713885307312}, {"word": " the", "start": 0.14, "end": 0.26, "probability": 0.9679760336875916}, {"word": " 60th", "start": 0.26, "end": 0.8, "probability": 0.9643579721450806}, {"word": " U", "start": 0.8, "end": 0.88, "probability": 0.6401295065879822}, {"word": ".S.", "start": 0.88, "end": 1.08, "probability": 0.9917712509632111}, {"word": " Secretary", "start": 1.3, "end": 1.34, "pr